#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084198} :activity/id #uuid "bb6800d8-7ba1-553c-9322-ca9069cbaa8a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051061 :topic/id #uuid "7de546e1-10a1-5134-abfc-9fe21121e7cb" :topic/math-academy-id 728} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084439 :tutorial/content "Our goal is to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can use **synthetic division**. Synthetic division allows us to divide a polynomial by a factor of the form $(x-c).$ Here's how we do it.\n\n| ![](images/86/8657b84c4cee9d122c5ec7fb62e652133c02b433e9bb73fb690573dd29857fe6.svg) | Start off by writing the term $c$ of the divisor on the left side. Here, $c = {\\color{blue}{3}}.$ Then, write the coefficients of all terms of the dividend on the right side in descending order of their degree. Remember the sign! Also, if a term is missing, use $0$ as the coefficient. |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](images/89/89ff1cd5f573ae387a716a8a446cd0867dfd464df0d9d9d91d4e9fd1f17f31ca.svg) | Bring down the leading term of the dividend. Here, the leading term is ${\\color{red}2}.$ |\n| ![](images/80/80e8e2f6092444058ec459f28518d0bd66b47858b9605562f8041aa09acda45e.svg) | Multiply the leading term $\\color{red}2$ by ${\\color{blue}3 }.$ This gives ${{\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot \\color{red}{2}} = {\\color{green}6}.$ Place the result below the next term. |\n| ![](images/a4/a44903e5fb107c1737c9ca1a6d0ba869784c7443b81068591bdb4ac0f7489555.svg) | Add the last result ($\\color{green}{6}$) to the term located above ($\\color{magenta}{-5}$) and write their sum below the line. This gives ${\\color{magenta}{-5}} + {\\color{green}{6}}={\\color{red}{1}}.$ |\n| ![](images/a6/a62d39bda1c2e3463ec07179055c47ac775369e9c0065725b9b607f70f56b68b.svg) | We now repeat the process. Multiply the last result ($\\color{red}1$ here) by $\\color{blue} 3$ and place the result below the next term. This gives ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}= {\\color{green}{3}}.$ |\n| ![](images/73/73578072793a196046848a135a745d97d17a711987919e6cc8bb96cf9c9712e0.svg) | Now add the last result ($\\color{green}{3}$) to the term located above ($\\color{magenta}{-7}$) and write their sum below the line. This gives ${\\color{magenta}{-7}} + {\\color{green}{3}}={\\color{red}{-4}}.$ |\n| ![](images/7f/7fc91ddc948d8471c21fc38f7a4aca73204ca7ded9f5e867ae1dd2aa2dfde1f3.svg) | There is one last step. Again, multiply $\\color{blue} 3$ by the last result $\\color{red}-4$ and write it below the constant term. This gives ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot{\\color{red}{(-4)}}={\\color{green}-12}.$ Finally, add $\\color{green}-12$ to $\\color{magenta}11$ to get ${\\color{blue}{-1}},$ which is the remainder. The remainder $\\color{blue}-1$ is important, so we put a box around it. |\n\nThe numbers $2,$ $1$ and $-4$ in the bottom row are the coefficients of the **quotient**. So from left to right, the quotient is\n\n$$\n2x^2+1x-4.\n$$\n\nThe **remainder** is the value in the box, so ${\\color{blue}-1}$ in this case. \n\nFinally, we can write the full result of the division as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4+\\dfrac{\\color{blue}-1}{x-3} ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4-\\dfrac{1}{x-3}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a99cb4a9-8428-584f-bcab-c2806d04719d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3383 :tutorial/title "Dividing a Polynomial Using Synthetic Division"} :step/id #uuid "7e09c9d5-51c1-52a9-a8a2-2acf0b38cd4d" :step/math-academy-id 18899 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083203 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083004 :question/id #uuid "551cceca-a69f-57c1-b4b0-c8f7aedd3cdc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5146"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c47216a0-3e51-50bb-b7eb-f92592db5dbc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049538}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551308}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Polynomial by a Linear Binomial Using Synthetic Division"} :step/id #uuid "7b4de1ae-1152-53f5-a47b-143b708404f8" :step/math-academy-id 18901 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084090}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084440 :tutorial/content "We have already seen how synthetic division can be used to quickly divide a polynomial by a linear binomial with a leading coefficient of $1.$ \n\nIn fact, synthetic division can be used when the divisor is *any* linear binomial, like $(ax+b).$ We just factor out the leading coefficient $a,$ carry out the division in the standard way, and then include the leading coefficient in the final result.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "691913a1-b87e-59e2-b4f0-6a72bf964f18" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3384 :tutorial/title "Dividing By a General Linear Factor"} :step/id #uuid "270d71b2-4b1a-5d4d-a08f-5d2e45600ae7" :step/math-academy-id 18903 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084091}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083204 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083005 :question/id #uuid "6d179766-30a7-5cd9-ba38-2ee6f49d37b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5149"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b0a87cd-e9b5-5ca1-8132-b62a715264ac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051079}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551260}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Polynomial by a Linear Binomial With Leading Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "300c079c-b57a-5be9-a005-dc19f97ed3d8" :step/math-academy-id 18905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084198 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084466 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to divide two polynomials, such as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's get familiar with some terminology:\n\n- The ** dividend** is the polynomial that we want to divide, so $2x^3-5x^2-7x+11$ in this case.\n\n- The **divisor** is the polynomial that we want to divide the dividend by, so $x-3$ in this case.\n\n- The result of the division, which will be a polynomial, is called the **quotient**.\n\n- The **remainder** is what is left after the division.\n\nBefore we start, we need to check that the polynomials are written in standard form. In our case, the terms in both the numerator and denominator are in descending order of their degree, so we're ready to go!" :tutorial/id #uuid "0402dd10-0c81-5ad3-b813-6af9d4fe5fbc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3928 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c1e715a2-551a-5990-b6c4-823e40d008ec" :step/math-academy-id 23194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084088}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084483 :tutorial/content "Dividing $2x^3-5x^2-7x+11$ by $x-3,$ we obtained that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4-\\dfrac{1}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying both sides by $x-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}=(x-3)(2x^2+x-4)-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to check if we have done the division correctly, we can simply multiply the polynomials on the right-hand side and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)(2{x}^{2}+x-4)-1 & =x(2{x}^{2}+x-4)-3(2{x}^{2}+x-4)-1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-4x-6{x}^{2}-3x+12-1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}-7x+11\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2e9f9179-dc6d-5214-a258-b8b1d6900457" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4984 :tutorial/title "Checking the Division"} :step/id #uuid "cb6d802c-b294-5078-a70b-ca55476b9f22" :step/math-academy-id 31046 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084089}}] :activity/title "Dividing Polynomials Using Synthetic Division" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083423}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551403} :activity/id #uuid "87369fa2-1336-5ef5-ab81-fdcbdb07cf83" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051061 :topic/id #uuid "7de546e1-10a1-5134-abfc-9fe21121e7cb" :topic/math-academy-id 728} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551384 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15c18ebe-c675-5bcf-b34d-fee9f38573de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+15x+25+\\frac{100}{3x-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550415} {:answer/id #uuid "5afbc067-5499-538e-a8b6-7c624c3edc2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}-15x-50+\\frac{100}{3x-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550591} {:answer/id #uuid "62a672e0-4667-5d14-94ed-8c58ba311d8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}-9x+2-\\frac{100}{3x-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550605} {:answer/id #uuid "af6c1d59-c605-55b7-98a8-43b605decc44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+5x+25-\\frac{100}{3x-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550845} {:answer/id #uuid "d952f828-349f-5d26-a20c-bf66dfa362fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+9x+21+\\frac{100}{3x-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550955}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550415} :answer-field/id #uuid "a916e5ab-d2bf-5c35-99df-efcacc3f8e56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "343c6002-3a70-5903-a244-0acddb5859ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-73818" :question/problem "$\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3x-5}=$" :question/worked-solution "We can calculate $\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3x-5}$ using synthetic division.\n\nFor synthetic division to work, we need the coefficient of $x$ in the denominator to be equal to $1.$ So first, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3x-5} & =\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3(x-\\frac{5}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{(x-\\frac{5}{3})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process on $\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{(x-\\frac{5}{3})}$, we arrive at the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{4} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & 9 & 12 & 0 & 0 & -25 \\\\  & ↓ & 15 & 45 & 75 & 125 \\\\  & 9 & 27 & 45 & 75 & 100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{(x-\\frac{5}{3})} & =\\frac{1}{3}(9{x}^{3}+27{x}^{2}+45x+75+\\frac{100}{(x-\\frac{5}{3})}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+15x+25+\\frac{100}{3x-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3x-5}=3{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+15x+25+\\frac{100}{3x-5}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "340e3ed6-35e3-5d45-bca2-83d91ae792ee"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551386 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c1bd531-0de7-525b-8300-8cc89c022598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1-\\frac{3}{x+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550401} {:answer/id #uuid "23683e8c-85ff-58cb-b908-b24a1cd95133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+21+\\frac{3}{x+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550448} {:answer/id #uuid "30488cbf-33f4-5e0f-959a-d6e3b556522f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+12+\\frac{3}{x+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550485} {:answer/id #uuid "afba8a58-1017-5f18-bf57-fd6a18b0ee20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}-6-\\frac{3}{x+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550847} {:answer/id #uuid "d659d2fd-291e-5be2-ad56-cfd41202f459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+4-\\frac{3}{x+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550947}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550401} :answer-field/id #uuid "23231c31-724d-51d7-aa6f-f7ca2083833d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "84195c6c-e13d-58c7-8f62-b8364f00b637" :question/math-academy-id "q-18577" :question/problem "$\\frac{-{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+x+1}{x+4}=$" :question/worked-solution "We can calculate $\\frac{-{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+x+1}{x+4}$ using synthetic division.\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process, we arrive at the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{4} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -4 & -1 & -3 & 4 & 1 & 1 \\\\  & ↓ & 4 & -4 & 0 & -4 \\\\  & -1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+x+1}{x+4}=-{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1-\\frac{3}{x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "34f2531b-3118-5542-84d9-9e90e68fa910" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551384}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551403 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "169f6f65-5871-519b-a5b5-58af3670b1d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}-x+9-\\frac{4}{x+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550416} {:answer/id #uuid "1d1d54fd-b5da-555c-97be-008df659bd4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}+2x-12+\\frac{4}{x+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550430} {:answer/id #uuid "8260834a-79a3-569a-85ef-7bdd27d16f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}+5x+1+\\frac{4}{x+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550697} {:answer/id #uuid "96603170-09ef-5253-abcf-4c72c537b471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}+9x-1+\\frac{4}{x+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550750} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ca52e5-4fd3-53ba-8842-120a1f358cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}+10x+11-\\frac{4}{x+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550866}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550697} :answer-field/id #uuid "711a21ce-a7d7-5796-93ec-6bc218a77acc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8ccbfa58-d595-551b-aac0-8c85b253cfbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-18548" :question/problem "$\\frac{4{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+16x+7}{x+3}=$" :question/worked-solution "We can calculate $\\frac{4{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+16x+7}{x+3}$ using synthetic division.\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process, we arrive at the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{4} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & 4 & 12 & 5 & 16 & 7 \\\\  & ↓ & -12 & 0 & -15 & -3 \\\\  & 4 & 0 & 5 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+16x+7}{x+3}=4{x}^{3}+5x+1+\\frac{4}{x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5996360f-63a5-5e3f-a0ef-bac173ffcb52" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551386}}] :activity/title "Dividing Polynomials Using Synthetic Division" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550353}]}
